Last night, my mind constructed a lurid nethack-themed nightmare.

In most dreams, my brain's ability to imagine visually is sadly
limited, so much so that in most nethack-related dreams, I have little
yellow letter cs bouncing around the room, and I'm trying in vain to
convince the people around me that they're some sort of threat. This
dream, however, was different...

In this dream, I was at some sort of conference on magic. I had walked
from the main hotel housing the attendees, accompanied by a man and a
young (13 or so) girl, talking about some magic-related topic. We
heard some strange hissing noises in a side room and decided to
investigate.

Inside was a statue of a lich in the act of spellcasting, perhaps
summoning some creature. Surely a *statue* wasn't making such noises,
was it? My two acquaintances seemed oblivious of the looming dread
that posessed me. Artists, in general, do not make statues of
something as ugly as a lich, and not in such a anguished - yet
realistic - pose.

That's when the dread cockatrice pecked one of my friends, the man, by
surprise. He turned to stone before I could act. Thankfully, the girl,
despite never encountering such a horrible creature, had the sense to
slip a nearby burlap sack over the diminutive monster. Genius!

"It won't be touching anything now," she said, shaken but somewhat
relieved, as she tied the sack shut.

The burlap sack shook violently, but the cockatrice made no noise save
for a muffled, yet sibilant HISSSSSSSSS.... HIISSSSSS...  That same
venomous hiss we heard from the hallway now emanated from that sack.

Oh my goodness, I said, we must kill the vile thing immediately, lest
the hiss render us as cold and unmoving as that lich.

The little girl trembled. "Its HISS can do that?" WIth that, she
gashed the sack with a knife, skewering the creature. But as she did,
some blood splattered on her bare hand, and she turned to stone.

I broke down and cried, feeling thoroughly powerless. Some of the
speeches I had heard during the conference, however, shot into memory.
The alchemic healers, I recalled, were busying themselves with
learning an incantation known as "stone to flesh". I raced to the
healer's suite of the hotel (suite 9 or 10, I think), and frantically
banged on door after door, but all of the healers were away save for a
young man.

"It's an emergency. Can you perform 'stone to flesh?'" I cried.

"Yes, but keep in mind I'm an amateur."

"And what happens if an 'amateur' performs stone to flesh?"

"Well, sometimes a boulder doesn't become meat all the way through,
you know - "

"THESE ARE *PEOPLE*, NOT BOULDERS!"

His eyes widened. "How?"

I slapped my face in disgust, explained the whole situation, then had
an idea.

"Well then, could you, um, 'partially' un-stone the lich? We could
threaten him with the cockatrice corpse and have *him* unstone the
people. He should be a reasonably good spellcaster, you know."

But even *this* ignoramus was afraid of encountering a lich, little
though he knew the creatures.

.
.
.

and that's when I woke up.

I think I've played too much nethack.



